A 0.2 K requirement against a current 0.5-0.6 K floor is a real gap
(roughly 2.5-3x), and "bad reconstructions scattered across the full
stemp/mmwater space" is an important new data point — it means the
earlier cold/dry-tail story isn't the whole explanation. That pattern
(each single variable looks fine, but errors don't cluster in any of
them individually) is the classic signature of a joint-density
problem: your marginal coverage of stemp, mmwater, etc. is fine on
their own, but specific combinations of inputs are underrepresented,
even though each variable individually spans a good range. That's
invisible in single-variable scatter plots and only shows up when you
look at local density in the full multivariate space.

Diagnostic to confirm this before committing effort: you already have
the nearest-neighbor code from the leakage check — reuse it, but now
correlate NN distance with per-profile error instead:


  nnIdx = knnsearch(Xtrain, Xval, 'K', 1);
  nnDist = sqrt(sum((Xval - Xtrain(nnIdx,:)).^2, 2));

  figure(14) ; scatter(nnDist, perProfileRMSE, 10, 'filled');
  xlabel('distance to nearest training neighbor'); ylabel('per-profile RMSE (K)');


If the worst-reconstructed profiles are consistently the ones with the
largest nearest-neighbor distance (sparse local neighborhoods), that
confirms it's a joint-coverage gap, not something wrong with the model
— and it tells you exactly what kind of fix will actually work.

If that's confirmed, here's the key question that determines your best
path forward: is running your RT forward model on a new synthetic
atmospheric state cheap, even though obtaining new real profiles is
hard? If yes, there's a well-established way out of "no more training
profiles" that doesn't need new real-world data at all — it's
essentially how classical fast-RT regression coefficient sets
(e.g. RTTOV's) have historically been built:

Combinatorial/mix-and-match augmentation. Take your existing 38k
profiles' components — T from profile A, Q from profile B, O3 from
profile C — and recombine them into new, physically-plausible
synthetic atmospheric states that never existed as a single real
profile, but whose individual pieces are all realistic. Then run your
actual RT code on each new combination to get a genuine label. This
directly targets the sparse-joint-density problem, because you're not
adding random new points, you're specifically filling in combinations
of (T-shape, Q-shape, O3-shape, surf conditions) that your original
38k didn't happen to co-occur.

A few guardrails to keep it physical:

  Combine within similar climate regimes where reasonable (don't pair
  a tropical T profile with an arctic Q profile, which would never
  physically co-occur) — or better, deliberately combine across
  regimes specifically in the regions your diagnostic shows are
  sparse.

  Enforce basic physical consistency after recombination
  (e.g. relative humidity bounds, hydrostatic sanity) so you're not
  feeding your RT code garbage states.

  This can also be done as interpolation rather than swapping:
  linearly interpolate the full state vector between two existing
  profiles at several fractions (0.25, 0.5, 0.75) and run RT on each
  interpolated state — simpler to implement than full mix-and-match,
  and still densifies the joint space along the lines connecting your
  existing points.

This can multiply your effective training set well beyond 38k without
needing a single new real atmospheric measurement — the constraint was
"hard to get profiles," and this sidesteps that by manufacturing new
physically-valid states from the pieces you already have, as long as
the RT code itself isn't the bottleneck.

Secondary things worth doing alongside this, once you're generating more labeled data:

  Ensemble a handful of models (different random seeds, maybe
  different widths) and average their predictions — typically buys a
  modest reduction (order 10-20%) in RMSE on top of whatever the data
  augmentation gets you, since it averages out prediction
  variance. Won't get you from 0.5 to 0.2 alone, but combines well
  with more data.

  Once you have the augmented set, rerun the exact NN-distance-vs-RMSE
  diagnostic to confirm the sparse pockets actually got filled and the
  correlation flattened out — don't just check the average RMSE
  improved, confirm the mechanism you targeted actually closed.

Is running new synthetic states through your RT code something you can
do at reasonable cost? That's the load-bearing assumption behind this
whole direction.

