On PCA and linear combinations — good catch, and the answer clarifies something important:

PCA compressing your inputs (T, Q, O3 profiles) has nothing to do with
whether the network can predict the output correctly. Here's the
distinction: yes, if you linearly interpolate two profiles and then
compress the result with PCA, the new profile's EOF coefficients land
exactly on the straight line between the two originals' coefficients —
PCA is linear, so that's guaranteed. But PCA doesn't know anything
about radiance. The mapping from EOF coefficients to radiance is what
the network learns, and you already proved that mapping is strongly
nonlinear (linear baseline: 2.27 K, DNN: 0.62 K). So even though the
new profile's input representation sits exactly halfway between two
known points, its correct radiance does not sit halfway between the
two known radiances — computing it still requires running the actual
RT code, and that's a genuinely new (X, Y) pair the network hasn't
seen. PCA compresses geometry; it doesn't compute radiative transfer.

One real limitation worth flagging, though: pure linear interpolation
only ever adds points along the line segment between two existing
profiles — it densifies your existing envelope, but doesn't reach
genuinely novel combinations outside it. Given your finding that bad
reconstructions are scattered across stemp/mmwater with no clean
cluster, that smells more like combinatorial sparsity (specific
combinations undersampled) than "there's a gap between two existing
points" — so component swapping (T from profile A, Q from profile B,
keeping each individually physical) is likely to hit those gaps more
directly than pure interpolation. Worth doing both.

