Good instinct, but worth flagging one thing before you build it: you
found bad reconstructions scattered across low, high, and in-between
stemp/mmwater values — not clustered at the tails. If that's the
pattern, augmenting only the low/high extremes will fix part of the
problem but likely leave the "in-between" sparse pockets
untouched. I'd combine your tail-targeting idea with a more general
density-based selection, so you catch gaps wherever they actually are,
not just where you'd guess them to be.

Step 1 — find sparse regions in the training set itself, using k-NN
distance as a density proxy (same tool you already used for the
leakage check, just applied differently):

  kNN = 5;
  [~, nnDistTrain] = knnsearch(Xtrain, Xtrain, 'K', kNN+1);  % col 1 is self (dist=0)
  localDensity = mean(nnDistTrain(:,2:end), 2);               % larger = sparser neighborhood

  [~, sparseOrder] = sort(localDensity, 'descend');
  sparseIdx = sparseOrder(1:round(0.1*size(Xtrain,1)));        % sparsest 10%

Step 2 — also explicitly flag your stemp/mmwater tails, as you
proposed:

 stempTrain   = surfTemp(trainIdx);
 mmwaterTrain = columnWater(trainIdx);   % however you're computing this
 
 tailIdx = unique([find(stempTrain <= prctile(stempTrain,10)); ...
                   find(stempTrain >= prctile(stempTrain,90)); ...
                   find(mmwaterTrain <= prctile(mmwaterTrain,10)); ...
                   find(mmwaterTrain >= prctile(mmwaterTrain,90))]);
 
 candidatePool = unique([sparseIdx(:); tailIdx(:)]);

Step 3 — interpolate raw physical profiles (not the PCA/EOF-compressed
versions — you need the actual T/Q/O3/etc. arrays to feed back into
your RT code) between each candidate and its nearest real neighbor, so
the new point fills the actual local gap rather than connecting two
arbitrary sparse points:

  nPairs = 3000;
  fracs  = [0.25 0.5 0.75];
  newIdx = 1;
  
  for p = 1:nPairs
      i = candidatePool(randi(numel(candidatePool)));
      nnIdx = knnsearch(Xtrain, Xtrain(i,:), 'K', 6);
      j = nnIdx(randi([2 6]));   % one of i's 5 nearest neighbors, skipping self
  
      for f = fracs
          Tnew  = f*rawT(trainIdx(i),:)  + (1-f)*rawT(trainIdx(j),:);
          Qnew  = f*rawQ(trainIdx(i),:)  + (1-f)*rawQ(trainIdx(j),:);
          O3new = f*rawO3(trainIdx(i),:) + (1-f)*rawO3(trainIdx(j),:);
          % ... same pattern for CO2/CH4/N2O, surfTemp, surfPressure, viewAngle, emissivity
  
          newProfiles(newIdx) = struct('T',Tnew,'Q',Qnew,'O3',O3new); %#ok<SAGROW>
          newIdx = newIdx + 1;
      end
  end

One real risk worth checking before you run these through the RT code:
interpolating between two very different atmospheric states can
produce something unphysical — most commonly relative humidity pushed
above 100%, or a temperature/humidity combination that wouldn't occur
in nature. Cheap sanity check before spending RT-code time on them:

  % rough RH check using your existing Q/T -> es(T) saturation formula
  RHnew = computeRH(Tnew, Qnew, pLevels);   % whatever RH utility you have
  if any(RHnew(:) > 100.5)   % small tolerance for interpolation overshoot
      % skip or clip this synthetic profile
  end

Once you've generated and RT-labeled the batch, rerun the exact
NN-distance-vs-perProfileRMSE scatter from before on the new
validation split — that's what tells you whether this actually closed
the gap or whether the scattered-everywhere pattern needs yet another
angle.

