Step 3 will generate about 3000 x 3 = 9000 new profiles?

Yes, that's right — nPairs = 3000 pairs, each interpolated at 3
fractions (0.25, 0.5, 0.75), gives 9000 new synthetic profiles, on top
of your existing 38k.

A couple of things worth thinking about before committing to that
number, though:

1. candidatePool might be much smaller than 3000² worth of distinct
pairs. If your sparsest 10% + tails works out to, say, 4000-5000
profiles, nPairs = 3000 random draws from that pool (with a random
neighbor each time) will inevitably produce a lot of repeat or
near-repeat pairs — you won't get 3000 genuinely distinct
interpolation lines. Not fatal, but worth knowing you may be somewhat
over-sampling the same gaps rather than covering as much new territory
as the raw count suggests.

2. Whether 9000 is the right scale depends on cost per RT run, which
only you know. If your RT code is fast enough that generating 9000 is
trivial, there's no real downside to starting there and checking
results. If it's expensive, you could start smaller — e.g. nPairs =
1000 (3000 new profiles) — retrain, and check whether the
NN-distance-vs-RMSE correlation and the scattered-error pattern have
measurably improved before spending the budget on the rest. That gives
you a mid-course readout rather than committing the full budget blind.

3. Consider varying fracs per pair rather than fixing it at [0.25,
0.5, 0.75] for everyone. For pairs that are already fairly close
together (small NN distance), three interpolated points along that
line adds less new information than for pairs spanning a large gap. If
RT cost matters, you could allocate more interpolated points to the
widest gaps and fewer to the narrow ones — e.g. scale the number of
fractions per pair by that pair's distance.

